Day 0: The Great Escape


When the travel bug bites, it doesn’t care if you’ve got a full itinerary or just a weekend and a backpack full of dreams. With my family off enjoying their summer vacation in the homeland, I found myself in Hong Kong, dramatically staring out the window like a lonely soap opera character. So, I did what any self-respecting solo traveler would do—I plotted my escape. Flights? Too pricey. Time? Too short. Spirit? Unbreakable. Armed with my trusty mainland travel card and a thirst for adventure, I dove into the depths of the internet (and survived the rocky romance between Google and China) to uncover hidden gems within two hours of Shenzhen. After some serious blog sleuthing and image stalking, I landed on Shaoguan—an offbeat place that I was determined to explore. Tickets booked, backpack ready, and caffeine loaded, I was ready to turn my weekend into a whirlwind of discovery.

Friday evening, I clocked out of work and straight into adventure mode. First stop: Lo Wu station. But not before indulging in some classic window shopping—because nothing says “solo travel prep” like pretending you’ll buy that overpriced travel backpack. Now, here’s the real travel hack: I spent the night in an overnight spa. Yes, you read that right. If you get a massage, they let you crash on their surprisingly comfy couches for free. Budget travel meets bougie relaxation!


Day 1: Danxia Dreams, Stair Wars, and a Kung Fu Panda Moment


By 6 AM, I was up, freshened, and riding the first MTR to Shenzhen North Station like a man on a mission. The station was massive, the security check was smoother than my last Teams call, and the trains? Punctual to the second. I boarded my high-speed chariot to Shaoguan (reaching a highest speed of 307km/hr) , armed with a window seat and zero need for music or scrolling. Watching villages and misty hills blur past was oddly meditative—like nature’s own Netflix.

At exactly 9:10 AM, I stepped onto Shaoguan soil. And boom—the AC-to-36-degree-humidity transition hit me like a slap from Mother Nature herself. But I was undeterred. My first destination: the legendary Danxia Mountains Scenic Area. I summoned a cab via the Didi app, and in a record-breaking 1 minute and 30 seconds, an EV rolled up like a knight in shining armor. The 110 km ride took just over an hour and cost me a mere 120 Yuan. At this point, I was convinced: Shaoguan was already winning.

The ride to Danxia Mountains was like flipping through a live-action postcard— straight roads flanked by endless greenery, misty hills in the distance, and the occasional glimpse of rural life unfolding quietly under the morning sun. But here’s the twist: Shaoguan doesn’t do English. Not on signboards, not in conversations, not even in confused hand gestures. My savior? Google Translate, which quickly became my travel BFF. My phone was working overtime, translating menus, signs, and the occasional philosophical billboard. Power bank? Absolutely essential.

The car dropped me off right in front of the ticketing counter at Danxia Mountain Scenic Area, and I was ready to explore this UNESCO Heritage. A quick geography lesson: Danxia is part of the China Danxia World Heritage Site, famous for its dramatic red sandstone formations that look like nature’s version of abstract art. Think towering cliffs, quirky rock shapes, serene temples, and a river that snakes through the mountains like it’s on a scenic joyride.

Entry ticket? 100 Yuan. Inside, I hopped onto a free golf cart and reached the second ticketing center. There, I shelled out another 180 Yuan for a round-trip cable car ride and two boat rides. Pro tip: If you can read Mandarin, you can snag an all-inclusive ticket for 260 Yuan at the first counter and save a few bucks. But hey, what’s travel without a little trial-and-error tuition?

Armed with my trusty Google Translate and a healthy dose of optimism, I decoded the Chinese brochure’s suggested scenic route. First up: a cable car ride to Changlao Peak, the highest point in Danxia. The cabins were tiny, conjoined, and cozy enough to make you bond with strangers over shared gasps and awe.

Once at the top, I had three trail options. Naturally, I chose the one that sounded easiest: a 630-meter hike to Rudder Stone. What I didn’t realize was that this was horizontal distance. The vertical reality? Around 700–800 stairs. And there I was—a 100 kg, unfit guy with a 10 kg backpack, channeling my inner Po from Kung Fu Panda. I huffed, I puffed, and I climbed like a determined dumpling.

But oh, the summit! The panoramic view was worth every wheeze and wobble. On the way down, I met a group of sprightly 70-somethings who were climbing like it was a morning stroll. They even took photos with me like I was some kind of celebrity. Then came a couple with a baby in a carrier, casually ascending like they were on a Sunday walk. That was my cue to reevaluate my fitness goals.

Just when I thought I’d earned my cable car descent, I remembered the Linkin Park Song..."I tried so hard and got so far, In the end"...it should matter right? There were 3 trails in all. 
Trail #2: a 240-meter hike to Shao Yin Pavilion. More stairs. 200–300 stairs down and then up again. Worth it. The view was magnificent, the air was crisp, and the heat? Slightly more tolerable.

Trail #3 was a 1700-meter hike to Zhanglao Elder Peak. I looked at it, looked at myself, and said, “Nope.” Luckily, I could see the peak from Shao Yin Pavilion, so I mentally checked it off my list and moved on.

By now, I hadn’t eaten a single morsel. I settled for an ice cream. Then it was cable car down, shuttle bus to the next spot: Xianglong Lake Wharf. The bus doesn’t drop you at the wharf. It drops you near a forest, and you walk 15 minutes through dense greenery to reach it. At the wharf—serenity. A tiny boat bobbing on glass-like water, mountains standing guard in the background. I hopped on for a peaceful 10-minute ride to the next trail: 800 meters to Yinyuan Stone. Until you realize it’s 700 stairs. Again. The bamboo forest and mountain stream made the climb cooler.

At the top, I was… underwhelmed. No sweeping views, just a rock shaped like a female sex organ. Nature’s sense of humor, I guess. I descended, returned to the wharf, and considered calling it a day. But wait—there was still the scenic ferry ride and the hike to Yangyuan Stone. I grabbed another ice cream and powered on. The shuttle dropped me back at the second ticket counter, but no golf carts were available. So, I walked. In the sun. For 1.5 km.

The scenic ferry ride took us through all the remaining spots, slowing down at key photospots. Bonus feature: a cameraman on board who insisted on taking your photo and handing you a voucher. Flash that voucher back at the pier and voila – a free photo on a key ring! Want the deluxe version? Just 30 Yuan extra. We cruised past Yangyuan Stone(shaped like a male sex organ) and made a very grown-up decision: no more stairs.

Once the ferry docked, I summoned my trusty Didi cab, which arrived in 45 seconds. The ride to my hotel was another scenic 80 km journey—50 minutes of déjà vu in reverse.

I reached the Vienna International Hotel around 5 PM. Think European-style paintings, grand chandeliers, and murals so dramatic they could star in their own opera. I checked in, took a shower that felt like a spiritual cleanse, and devoured my first proper meal of the day and crashed into sleep for an hour. My phone buzzed with missed calls from my wife and friends, probably wondering if I’d been swallowed by a mountain.

At 7:30 PM, I re-emerged and wandered down to the pedestrian street, hoping for a foot massage. No luck, but the shops were charming and sold surprisingly affordablemerchandise. I treated myself to some local delicacies: a cucumber salad so fresh it could’ve starred in a spa commercial, and soupy noodles so flavorful I shamelessly licked the bowl. With a full belly and a happy heart, I returned to the hotel and called it a night — ending my Day 1 of epic, stair-filled, ice-cream-fueled solo adventure in Shaoguan.


Day 2: Coffee, Caves, Crickets and Calmness


After a sleep so deep, it could’ve been mistaken for hibernation, I woke up at 9 AM feeling like a new person—or at least a slightly less exhausted version of myself. First order of business: coffee. I found a sachet of Luckin Coffee in a local supermarket, which turned out to be a liquid concentrate miracle. Just add 200 ml of hot water and voilà—an Americano that could rival any overpriced café brew.

Checked out of the hotel around 10 AM and summoned my ever-reliable Didi. As usual, it arrived in under a minute, ready to whisk me off to my next destination: Maba Man Ruins. The ride gave me a lovely glimpse of Shaoguan’s quaint charming town split by the Zhen Jiang River, connected by beautiful bridges and dotted with a mix of modern and old architecture. Shops here don’t rush to open or stay open late. It’s the kind of place that politely tells hustle culture to take a hike.

The car dropped me off at The Shixia Site of the Maba Man, and I was instantly hit with picnic vibes. Families, greenery, and a peaceful atmosphere. First stop: the Cave of Maba Man.

History Break! In June 1958, local farmers discovered fossilized skulls here, later identified as belonging to a 129,000-year-old early Paleolithic Archaic Homo Sapiens—formally named Maba Man. In 2000, this site was declared one of the 100 greatest archaeological discoveries of 20th-century China. Fossils of nearly 40 mammal species were found here—including porcupines, bears, pandas, hyenas, tapirs, rhinos, deer, saber-toothed elephants, and orangutans.

nside the cave, statues depicted Maba Man’s daily life—hunting, cooking, and caring for children. A 400-stair climb to the top of Lion Rock gave me a bird’s-eye view of Shaoguan. It was humbling to stand in a place so rich in history and human legacy.  After exiting the cave and circling the mountain, I explored the rest of the site. The pathway was serene, shaded by tall trees and scattered with dried leaves. Every 50 meters, a speaker disguised as a rock played soft, soothing music and there was the natural sound of crickets. It felt like walking through nature’s own spa.  I met a few families along the way who tried to chat, but the language barrier was real. Enter: Google Translate’s microphone feature. It saved the day and turned awkward smiles into actual conversations. Solo travel win!

Next up: Zhaoyin Temple, Sigiriya Lion Rock Springs, and finally, the Village of the Maba Man. Replica huts and statues showcased prehistoric life. One scene had ten Maba men hunting a sabre-toothed elephant stuck in mud. Another showed women caring for children while men cooked using fire. What struck me most was the portrayal of gender equality in prehistoric family life. Respect all around.

The on-site museum was a cherry on top—well-organized, informative (though mostly in Chinese), and packed with fossil replicas, artifacts, and interactive displays. It was educational, engaging, and a perfect wrap-up to the Maba Man chapter of my journey.

With my brain full of ancient wisdom and my legs still recovering from yesterday’s stair marathon, I booked a Didi to my next destination: Nanhua Temple. After immersing myself in prehistoric life, I was ready to time-travel forward—just a little—to the peaceful world of Nanhua Temple. Entry ticket? A humble 20 Yuan. But what awaited inside was priceless.

The moment I stepped in, I was wrapped in a blanket of calm. Lotus flowers floated in serene ponds, incense smoke curled through the air like slow-motion poetry, and Buddhist chants echoed softly in the background. The temple wasn’t flashy—it was simple, elegant, and deeply spiritual.

I’m an atheist at heart, but standing among centuries-old relics and sacred halls, I learned something profound: humility in greatness. The temple housed incredible religious artifacts and statues, yet everything felt grounded, peaceful, and welcoming.

Outside, a vegetarian restaurant offered a quiet bite, which I decided to skip being a hard-core non-vegetarian guy myself. Incense sticks were free for anyone who wanted to make an offering. Doves and pigeons fluttered around like little messengers of peace. But of course, this being Shaoguan, the temples were built on a slope—so yes, more stairs.

After soaking in the tranquility, I decided to take a long 1.5 km walk to a nearby pagoda. Sadly, it was closed for renovation, but I managed to snap a few photos for the travel diary. With my legs officially filing for retirement and my heart full of stories, I booked a Didi to Shaoguan Railway Station for the return journey to Shenzhen.

While I was sweating buckets and climbing staircases that felt like spiritual tests in Shaoguan, back in Hong Kong, Typhoon Wipha was throwing a full-blown tantrum. A T10-level typhoon had grounded all flights, shaken up weekend plans, and turned the city into a wind tunnel. But somehow, my journey back was smooth as silk. The train from Shaoguan to Shenzhenbei, and then from Shenzhenbei to West Kowloon in Hong Kong, ran like clockwork—no delays, no drama. And as if the universe was giving me a gentle pat on the back, Hong Kong greeted me with clear skies when I arrived.


Reflections of a Solo Traveler


This trip wasn’t just about ticking off scenic spots or surviving Shaoguan’s legendary staircases. It was about rediscovering solitude, embracing discomfort, and learning to listen—to nature, to history, and to myself.

I learned that humility lives in the quietest places—in ancient temples, in prehistoric caves, and in the smiles of strangers who speak a different language. I realized that strength isn’t always loud; sometimes it’s just the quiet determination to climb one more stair, take one more step, or eat one more ice cream.

Solo travel strips away the noise. It leaves you with your thoughts, your limits, and your curiosity. And somewhere between the bamboo forests and the boat rides, I found a little more of myself.

Until the next adventure—stay curious, stay humble, and never underestimate the power of a good pair of walking shoes.
